00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:37,280
So one of the main applications of game perfection is what's called the entry game.

12
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:42,080
So in the entry game, we basically picture two scenarios that I like to go with.

13
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:43,080
There are two firms.

14
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:45,480
One of the firms is going to be an entrant.

15
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:52,200
So there's an entrant, and the entrant can decide to either go in or out of a market.

16
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:57,800
And then, if the entrant stays out, then the incumbent has a nice monopoly.

17
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:03,920
So the entrant gets zero profits, and the monopolist gets a good profit, say, 10.

18
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:07,920
On the other hand, if the entrant goes in, then the monopolist has two options.

19
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:12,880
He can either fight the entrant, or he can accommodate the entrant.

20
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:19,920
So he can either go very competitive and try to punish the entrant, or he can just go and

21
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:22,520
say, fine, I'll have to share the market with you.

22
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:27,920
So in the scenario where there's a fight, then say both firms will have losses.

23
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:32,800
So they compete down to marginal costs, say, their fixed costs are negative 10, so they

24
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:33,800
both lose 10.

25
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:37,360
Then on the other hand, in the accommodation scenario, that's one where they share the

26
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:41,120
